 "grammar": {
  "title": {
   "ko": "부사: 정도 부사와 방법 부사",
   "en": "adverbs of degree and manner: quite, really, very, fast"
  },
  "explain": {
   "ko": "very, quite, really, too는 형용사나 부사 앞에서 정도를 나타냅니다. 방법 부사는 동사를 꾸미며 보통 형용사 + -ly로 만듭니다 (slow → slowly, careful → carefully). fast, hard처럼 형태가 같은 부사도 있고, good의 부사는 well입니다.",
   "en": "Very, quite, really and too go before an adjective or adverb to show degree. Manner adverbs describe a verb and are often adjective + -ly (slow → slowly, careful → carefully). Some adverbs keep the same form (fast, hard) and the adverb of good is well."
  },
  "table": {
   "head": [
    "표현 / Pattern",
    "예 / Example"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "very / quite / really",
     "The film was very interesting."
    ],
    [
     "too + 형용사",
     "The coffee is too hot."
    ],
    [
     "형용사 + -ly",
     "Drive carefully. Speak slowly."
    ],
    [
     "fast / hard / well",
     "He runs fast. She speaks English well."
    ]
   ]
  },
  "notes": [
   {
    "ko": "정도 부사는 형용사 앞.",
    "en": "Degree adverbs go before the adjective."
   },
   {
    "ko": "fast, hard는 형용사와 부사가 같은 모양.",
    "en": "Fast and hard are the same as adjective and adverb."
   },
   {
    "ko": "good → well.",
    "en": "good → well."
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   {
    "t": "The film was very interesting.",
    "ko": "그 영화는 아주 재미있었어요."
   },
   {
    "t": "The exam was quite difficult.",
    "ko": "그 시험은 꽤 어려웠어요."
   },
   {
    "t": "He runs fast.",
    "ko": "그는 빨리 달려요."
   },
   {
    "t": "Drive carefully.",
    "ko": "조심해서 운전하세요."
   },
   {
    "t": "She speaks English well.",
    "ko": "그녀는 영어를 잘해요."
   }
  ],
